  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 150 AM CST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\nWith dry air remaining over the Quad State, temperatures will\nlikely continue to overperform today and even Sunday over the\nsoutheast half of the region. Low to mid 70s will be the rule\nagain this afternoon and that could approach record levels in\nsome locations. The record of 71 at KCGI is likely to fall. For\nSunday, the records at KCGI and KPAH could be in jeopardy before\nthe rain arrives.\n\nSpeaking of the rain, a band of showers is expected to develop\nahead of a rather strong cold front and move eastward across the\nQuad State mainly during the day Sunday. Rainfall amounts will\nrange from around a quarter inch over southeast Missouri and\nsouthern Illinois to a tenth of an inch or less over the\nPennyrile of west Kentucky. The GFS and ECMWF continue bring\nsome very meager MUCAPE up into southeast Missouri around midday\nSunday, so a rumble of thunder cannot be ruled out. The presence\nof any instability could lead to a few locations getting a quarter\nto a half inch of rainfall.\n\nThe strong cold front will take temperatures down a few degrees\nbelow normal for Monday which will be 15-20 degrees lower than\nSunday's levels. With persistent north winds, temperatures will\ncontinue to trend lower with upper 40s to lower 50s the norm\nfrom Tuesday through at least Friday. \n\nThe medium range guidance continues to display considerable\nvariability in the potential for rainfall Wednesday through the\nThanksgiving weekend. However, there has been a rather \npersistent signal for rain to spread north over at least the \neastern half of the Quad State Tuesday night into Wednesday. \nThis WPC forecast now has 20-50% chances over the eastern half \nof the Quad State, with the best chances over the southern \nPennyrile. Only light QPF of a tenth of an inch or less is\ncurrently forecast.\n\nHowever, the 00Z ensembles are considerably more bullish in this\ntime frame. The 00Z GEFS has a 50-90% chance of rain over all \nbut the western half of southeast Missouri. The best chances are\nover the southern Pennyrile, where it even has a 40% chance of \n0.5\" of rainfall. The 00Z ECENS is not quite so bullish, but \ndoes have a 50-90% chance mainly over west Kentucky and \nsouthwest Indiana. The bottom line is that the chance of rain \nover eastern portions of the Quad State is likely to increase \nfor late Tuesday night through Wednesday, and there is a chance\nof some substantial rainfall, at least over the southern \nPennyrile.",
